@module flutter-m03 | Layout and UI
@summary Arrange widgets with containers, rows, columns, stacks, lists, grids, slivers and responsive layouts.

@lesson flutter-m03-l01 | Container | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use Container for padding, colour, size and decoration
- Know when a lighter widget is better

## The Swiss-army box
`Container` combines padding, margin, size constraints, alignment and decoration in one widget. It is convenient for cards, badges and coloured boxes.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Badge2 extends StatelessWidget {
  const Badge2({super.key});
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Container(
      margin: const EdgeInsets.all(8),
      padding: const EdgeInsets.symmetric(horizontal: 12, vertical: 6),
      decoration: BoxDecoration(
        color: Colors.indigo,
        borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(16),
        boxShadow: const [BoxShadow(blurRadius: 6, color: Colors.black26)],
      ),
      child: const Text('NEW', style: TextStyle(color: Colors.white)),
    );
  }
}
```

`color` and `decoration` cannot be used together; put the colour inside `BoxDecoration` when you need both. If you only need spacing, prefer `Padding` or `SizedBox`: they are lighter and can be `const`.

## Summary
- Container = padding + margin + constraints + decoration + alignment.
- Use `BoxDecoration` for rounded corners, borders, gradients and shadows.
- Prefer simpler widgets when you need only one feature.

@lesson flutter-m03-l02 | Padding | 6
## Learning objectives
- Add space inside widgets with Padding and EdgeInsets

## Space around content
`Padding` insets its child. `EdgeInsets` describes the amounts:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

const p1 = EdgeInsets.all(16);
const p2 = EdgeInsets.symmetric(horizontal: 24, vertical: 8);
const p3 = EdgeInsets.only(left: 12, top: 4);

Widget padded() => const Padding(padding: p2, child: Text('Hello'));
```

Use multiples of 4 or 8 (4, 8, 12, 16, 24) for a consistent rhythm. For right-to-left languages use `EdgeInsetsDirectional.only(start: 12)` so spacing flips automatically.

## Summary
- `Padding` adds inner space; `EdgeInsets.all/symmetric/only` define it.
- Use a spacing scale and `EdgeInsetsDirectional` for RTL support.

@lesson flutter-m03-l03 | SizedBox | 6
## Learning objectives
- Give widgets fixed sizes and add gaps with SizedBox

## Fixed sizes and gaps
`SizedBox` forces a width and/or height. Empty SizedBoxes are the standard way to add a gap in a Row or Column.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget form() => const Column(children: [
      TextField(decoration: InputDecoration(labelText: 'Email')),
      SizedBox(height: 16),
      SizedBox(width: double.infinity, child: FilledButton(onPressed: null, child: Text('Sign in'))),
    ]);
```

`SizedBox.expand()` fills all available space and `SizedBox.shrink()` takes no space, which is useful as an "empty" widget.

## Summary
- `SizedBox(height: 16)` is the idiomatic gap.
- `width: double.infinity` makes a child as wide as its parent allows.

@lesson flutter-m03-l04 | Row | 8
## Learning objectives
- Lay out children horizontally
- Control alignment on the main and cross axes

## Horizontal layout
`Row` places children left to right (right to left in RTL locales).

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget header() => const Row(
      mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.spaceBetween,
      crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.center,
      children: [
        Icon(Icons.menu),
        Text('Bimo'),
        Icon(Icons.notifications_none),
      ],
    );
```

- **Main axis** (horizontal for Row): `start`, `center`, `end`, `spaceBetween`, `spaceAround`, `spaceEvenly`.
- **Cross axis** (vertical): `start`, `center`, `end`, `stretch`.

A Row does not scroll. If children are too wide you get the yellow-and-black overflow stripe; fix it with `Expanded`, `Flexible`, `Wrap` or a scroll view.

## Summary
- Row lays out horizontally; align with `mainAxisAlignment` and `crossAxisAlignment`.
- Overflow means children are wider than the space; use Expanded or Wrap.

@lesson flutter-m03-l05 | Column | 8
## Learning objectives
- Stack children vertically and control their size

## Vertical layout
`Column` is the vertical twin of Row.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget profile() => const Column(
      mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min,
      crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start,
      children: [
        Text('Ada Lovelace', style: TextStyle(fontSize: 22, fontWeight: FontWeight.bold)),
        SizedBox(height: 4),
        Text('Level 7'),
      ],
    );
```

`mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min` makes the Column only as tall as its children (useful inside dialogs and cards). A Column inside a scrollable needs this or a bounded height. For long content, use `ListView` or wrap the Column in `SingleChildScrollView`.

## Summary
- Column lays out vertically; `mainAxisSize.min` shrink-wraps it.
- Use scroll views when content may be taller than the screen.

@lesson flutter-m03-l06 | Stack | 8
## Learning objectives
- Overlap widgets and position them with Positioned

## Layers
`Stack` paints children on top of each other: the first child at the bottom.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget avatarWithBadge() => const Stack(
      clipBehavior: Clip.none,
      children: [
        CircleAvatar(radius: 32, child: Icon(Icons.person)),
        Positioned(
          right: -2,
          bottom: -2,
          child: CircleAvatar(radius: 10, backgroundColor: Colors.green),
        ),
      ],
    );
```

Non-positioned children are aligned with `alignment` (top-left by default). `Positioned` places a child relative to the Stack's edges. Use Stack for badges, image overlays, gradients over photos and floating controls.

## Summary
- Stack overlaps children; later children paint on top.
- `Positioned` sets offsets; `Positioned.fill` covers the whole Stack.

@lesson flutter-m03-l07 | Expanded | 6
## Learning objectives
- Share remaining space between Row/Column children

## Fill the remaining space
`Expanded` makes a child of a Row, Column or Flex take the remaining space along the main axis. With several Expanded children, `flex` sets the ratio.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget splitBar() => Row(children: [
      Expanded(flex: 2, child: Container(height: 8, color: Colors.green)),
      Expanded(flex: 1, child: Container(height: 8, color: Colors.grey)),
    ]);
```

A common fix for overflow: wrap a long `Text` in a Row with `Expanded` so it wraps or ellipsizes instead of overflowing.

## Summary
- Expanded fills remaining main-axis space; `flex` controls ratios.
- It must be a direct child of Row, Column or Flex.

@lesson flutter-m03-l08 | Flexible | 6
## Learning objectives
- Let children grow up to their share without forcing them to fill it

## Flexible vs Expanded
`Flexible` gives a child *up to* its share of space; the child may be smaller. `Expanded` is simply `Flexible(fit: FlexFit.tight)`.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget chipRow() => const Row(children: [
      Flexible(child: Text('A short label', overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis)),
      SizedBox(width: 8),
      Icon(Icons.chevron_right),
    ]);
```

Here the text only takes what it needs but ellipsizes if the row is too narrow.

## Summary
- Flexible = may be smaller than its share; Expanded = must fill it.

@lesson flutter-m03-l09 | Wrap | 6
## Learning objectives
- Flow children onto new lines when space runs out

## Wrapping content
`Wrap` is like a Row that continues on the next line, perfect for tags and chips.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget tags(List<String> items) => Wrap(
      spacing: 8,
      runSpacing: 8,
      children: [for (final t in items) Chip(label: Text(t))],
    );
```

`spacing` is the gap between items, `runSpacing` the gap between lines.

## Summary
- Wrap avoids overflow by flowing children onto new runs.

@lesson flutter-m03-l10 | Align | 6
## Learning objectives
- Position a child inside its parent with Alignment

## Alignment
`Align` positions its child using `Alignment(x, y)` where -1 is start and 1 is end.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget corner() => const SizedBox(
      height: 120,
      child: Align(alignment: Alignment.bottomRight, child: Icon(Icons.star)),
    );
```

Named constants include `topLeft`, `center`, `bottomRight`; `AlignmentDirectional.centerStart` respects RTL.

## Summary
- Align places a child within the available space using Alignment values.

@lesson flutter-m03-l11 | Center | 4
## Learning objectives
- Centre a child horizontally and vertically

## Centring
`Center` is `Align(alignment: Alignment.center)`. It expands to fill its parent (when constraints allow) and centres its child.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget loading() => const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator());
```

Use `widthFactor`/`heightFactor` to make Center size itself relative to its child instead of expanding.

## Summary
- Center centres its child; it is a shortcut for Align with Alignment.center.

@lesson flutter-m03-l12 | ListView | 10
## Learning objectives
- Build scrolling lists efficiently with ListView.builder

## Scrolling lists
For a few fixed items use `ListView(children: [...])`. For long or dynamic lists use `ListView.builder`, which only builds the items currently visible.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class LessonList extends StatelessWidget {
  const LessonList({super.key, required this.titles});
  final List<String> titles;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return ListView.separated(
      itemCount: titles.length,
      separatorBuilder: (_, __) => const Divider(height: 1),
      itemBuilder: (context, i) => ListTile(
        leading: CircleAvatar(child: Text('${i + 1}')),
        title: Text(titles[i]),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

Give items stable keys (`ValueKey(item.id)`) when the list can reorder. `ListView` inside a Column must be wrapped in `Expanded` so it has a bounded height.

## Summary
- `ListView.builder`/`separated` build lazily and scale to thousands of items.
- Unbounded height errors mean the ListView needs Expanded or a fixed height.

@lesson flutter-m03-l13 | GridView | 8
## Learning objectives
- Display items in a responsive grid

## Grids
`GridView.builder` lays items out in a lazy grid. `SliverGridDelegateWithMaxCrossAxisExtent` adapts the column count to the screen width.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget courseGrid(List<String> names) => GridView.builder(
      padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),
      gridDelegate: const SliverGridDelegateWithMaxCrossAxisExtent(
        maxCrossAxisExtent: 200,
        mainAxisSpacing: 12,
        crossAxisSpacing: 12,
        childAspectRatio: 1,
      ),
      itemCount: names.length,
      itemBuilder: (_, i) => Card(child: Center(child: Text(names[i]))),
    );
```

Use `WithFixedCrossAxisCount` when you always want, say, two columns.

## Summary
- GridView.builder is lazy; MaxCrossAxisExtent makes grids responsive.

@lesson flutter-m03-l14 | CustomScrollView | 8
## Learning objectives
- Combine several scrollable sections into one scroll

## One scroll, many sections
`CustomScrollView` scrolls a list of **slivers** together, for example a collapsing header, a grid and a list.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget page() => CustomScrollView(slivers: [
      const SliverAppBar.large(title: Text('Courses')),
      SliverList.builder(
        itemCount: 20,
        itemBuilder: (_, i) => ListTile(title: Text('Lesson ${i + 1}')),
      ),
    ]);
```

Nesting a ListView inside another ListView is a common mistake; use a CustomScrollView with slivers instead.

## Summary
- CustomScrollView scrolls multiple slivers as one surface.

@lesson flutter-m03-l15 | Slivers | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use common slivers: SliverAppBar, SliverList, SliverGrid, SliverToBoxAdapter

## Scrollable building blocks
Slivers are pieces of a scroll view that lay themselves out lazily.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget dashboard() => CustomScrollView(slivers: [
      const SliverAppBar(pinned: true, expandedHeight: 160, flexibleSpace: FlexibleSpaceBar(title: Text('Home'))),
      const SliverToBoxAdapter(child: Padding(padding: EdgeInsets.all(16), child: Text('Recommended'))),
      SliverGrid.count(crossAxisCount: 2, children: [for (var i = 0; i < 4; i++) Card(child: Center(child: Text('Card $i')))]),
      const SliverFillRemaining(hasScrollBody: false, child: Center(child: Text('The end'))),
    ]);
```

`SliverToBoxAdapter` puts a normal widget into a sliver list; `pinned` keeps the app bar visible.

## Summary
- Slivers enable collapsing headers and mixed lazy content.

@lesson flutter-m03-l16 | Responsive layout | 8
## Learning objectives
- Adapt layouts to phones, tablets and desktops

## Breakpoints
Responsive apps change layout by available width. Material guidance uses window size classes: compact (< 600 dp), medium (600–839), expanded (≥ 840).

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class AdaptiveHome extends StatelessWidget {
  const AdaptiveHome({super.key});
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    final width = MediaQuery.sizeOf(context).width;
    if (width >= 840) {
      return const Row(children: [NavigationRail(destinations: [
        NavigationRailDestination(icon: Icon(Icons.home), label: Text('Home')),
        NavigationRailDestination(icon: Icon(Icons.book), label: Text('Courses')),
      ], selectedIndex: 0), Expanded(child: Center(child: Text('Content')))]);
    }
    return const Center(child: Text('Phone layout'));
  }
}
```

Avoid hard-coded pixel sizes; let `Expanded`, `Flexible`, `Wrap` and grids adapt.

## Summary
- Pick layouts by width classes; use NavigationRail on wide screens.

@lesson flutter-m03-l17 | MediaQuery | 6
## Learning objectives
- Read screen size, padding and text scale

## Device metrics
`MediaQuery` exposes information about the screen. Use the specific accessors so widgets rebuild only when needed.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget info(BuildContext context) {
  final size = MediaQuery.sizeOf(context);
  final insets = MediaQuery.paddingOf(context);   // status bar, notch
  final keyboard = MediaQuery.viewInsetsOf(context).bottom;
  return Text('${size.width.round()}x${size.height.round()} • top ${insets.top} • keyboard $keyboard');
}
```

Respect the user's text scaling (`MediaQuery.textScalerOf`) instead of fixing font sizes.

## Summary
- `sizeOf`, `paddingOf`, `viewInsetsOf` read screen metrics efficiently.

@lesson flutter-m03-l18 | LayoutBuilder | 6
## Learning objectives
- Build based on the parent's constraints rather than the screen size

## Constraint-aware widgets
`LayoutBuilder` gives you the parent's `BoxConstraints`. This is better than MediaQuery for reusable components, which may be placed in a narrow sidebar on a wide screen.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget card() => LayoutBuilder(builder: (context, constraints) {
      final wide = constraints.maxWidth > 400;
      return wide
          ? const Row(children: [Icon(Icons.school, size: 48), SizedBox(width: 16), Text('Wide card')])
          : const Column(children: [Icon(Icons.school, size: 48), Text('Narrow card')]);
    });
```

Remember Flutter's rule: constraints go down, sizes go up, parent sets position.

## Summary
- LayoutBuilder adapts a widget to the space it is actually given.
